1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,333
Most trading agents are built to trade.

2
00:00:03,333 --> 00:00:05,533
This one is built to refuse.

3
00:00:05,533 --> 00:00:12,133
Over the live week it evaluated 81 real opportunities and declined 95 percent of them,

4
00:00:12,133 --> 00:00:17,000
and every refusal is a signed artifact you can verify yourself.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,900
Let me show you why that is the point, not a bug.

6
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:23,967
The thesis is the volatility risk premium:

7
00:00:23,967 --> 00:00:27,967
SPY options are priced richer than the movement that follows.

8
00:00:27,967 --> 00:00:32,600
Before a single line of backtest code, I pre-registered the hypotheses,

9
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:35,467
the trial count, and a multiple-testing bar.

10
00:00:35,467 --> 00:00:37,800
The git history proves the order.

11
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:41,167
Nearly seven years of SPY, out of sample:

12
00:00:41,167 --> 00:00:46,667
mean premium 3.68 vol points over 1,741 observations, Newey-West t of 4.74.

13
00:00:46,667 --> 00:00:50,967
The naive t of 18 is invalid, because the windows overlap.

14
00:00:50,967 --> 00:00:54,300
The edge is real, and the number is honest.

15
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:58,200
The agent sells that premium with defined-risk iron condors,

16
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:03,233
only after eleven numbered gates pass: position integrity, timing, circuit breakers,

17
00:01:03,233 --> 00:01:07,800
a contango filter, the premium threshold at the exact strikes it would sell,

18
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:10,833
event proximity, cost, sizing, and a stagger rule.

19
00:01:10,833 --> 00:01:16,000
Every call goes through Alpaca's official MCP server, request and response recorded.

20
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:20,500
The deployed tenor was not picked from the backtest ranking.

21
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:25,700
Three candidates raced live in parallel for eight days on real market data,

22
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:26,867
risking nothing.

23
00:01:26,867 --> 00:01:32,100
The one that actually traded won: two days of five, against zero and one.

24
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:36,667
Every decision, fill and refusal is hashed into a Merkle tree,

25
00:01:36,667 --> 00:01:38,967
sealed before outcomes are known.

26
00:01:38,967 --> 00:01:41,200
One command recomputes the root.

27
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:46,600
If anything had been edited after the fact, this line would not say verified.

28
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:50,300
Here is the strongest evidence that this works.

29
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:55,633
On the first live morning, a payload-parsing bug read the account as flat,

30
00:01:55,633 --> 00:01:59,267
and the agent stacked four condors instead of one.

31
00:01:59,267 --> 00:02:01,100
The sealed log caught it:

32
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:05,567
one artifact holds both the raw broker response with the legs,

33
00:02:05,567 --> 00:02:08,567
and the reconciliation that ignored them.

34
00:02:08,567 --> 00:02:13,967
Bug, fix, regression tests and the adopted positions are all in the record.

35
00:02:13,967 --> 00:02:15,300
I did not hide it.

36
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:17,500
The record would not let me.

37
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:19,000
Live P&L so far:

38
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:24,100
plus 36 dollars and 93 cents on a hundred thousand dollar paper account.

39
00:02:24,100 --> 00:02:30,067
On Friday a deadline flatten closes every position ninety minutes before submission.

40
00:02:30,067 --> 00:02:31,633
Realised, not marked.

41
00:02:31,633 --> 00:02:36,333
One week of options P&L is mostly noise, and the write-up says so.

42
00:02:36,333 --> 00:02:41,867
The tail hedge is coded and never engaged: Alpaca served no VIX data all week.

43
00:02:41,867 --> 00:02:44,800
That refusal is logged every cycle too.

44
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:45,900
Clone it.

45
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:49,233
Run make test, make verify, make summary.

46
00:02:49,233 --> 00:02:51,533
You do not have to trust me.

47
00:02:51,533 --> 00:02:54,700
That is the whole design.
